Following the principles of observable interactions between humans and machines in a HMS framework as suggested by Degani (1996) , the proposed framework identifies five possible in-vehicle interactions in MPDD from the perspective of a HMS: human-car interface, human-car controls, human-mobile phone interface, human-mobile phone control, and human-environment interface (see Fig. 1 (b)). Each of these in-vehicle interactions has different tasks and associated performance measures ; the details of these interactions are summarized in Table 1 . For instance, talking on a mobile phone while driving is an in-vehicle task set in the human-mobile phone interface, which can eventually affect the system properties (described later) as well as the performance of the remaining four interactions. Additionally, some mobile phone tasks concurrently involve two interactions, in particular mobile phone control and mobile phone interface. For example, texting, and dialling using the tactile interface requires a driver to simultaneously use the mobile phone display and controls. This distinction is important for recognizing the complexity of the tasks, and the need for a driver to allocate additional resources when a concurrent interaction with control and interface takes place.

