The in-vehicle tasks shown in Fig. 1 (b) are comprised of two tasks: driving and using a mobile phone. These tasks can impact on each other; this causal influence can be described as ‘interference between in-vehicle tasks’. The interference is a two-way interaction between the mobile phone task and the driving task. The typical approach in the literature has been to examine how the mobile phone task affects driving tasks such as navigation, response to road traffic events, brake pedal movements, and steering wheel controls. The inverse relationship—the influence of the driving task on secondary tasks like a mobile phone conversation, has also been reported in terms of changes in the performance of the secondary task while driving.
Together, changes in system components and interference between in-vehicle tasks have an impact on system outcomes. The main outcome of a HMS relates to its functional requirements or functionality. In the MPDD context, a system’s functionality is related to mobility and is generally measured with parameters that describe the movement of the vehicle, such as acceleration, headway, lane position, and speed. In addition to functionality, the system outcomes have lifecycle system properties or “ilities” ( De Weck et al., 2011 ). de Weck et al. (2012) have identified at least 15 lifecycle system properties including quality, reliability, flexibility safety, durability, resiliency , robustness and evolvability. Theoretically, every system should have all of these possible lifecycle system properties or ilities, and the same is true for MPDD. This study only reported five lifecycle systems properties (also called system properties as used in this paper) for MPDD as a HMS, as these were identified as a product of this review. Table 2 includes both the original and adapted definitions of these five system properties. Examples of system outcomes against each system property are also included in Table 2 .

