3. Methodology and research protocol
Applying the above HMS research lens to examine MPDD, a systematic literature review was conducted. Given the large amount of components and causal mechanisms theoretically described in the HMS, a systematic classification scheme (SCS) was developed to guide the literature review and to enable an assessment of the degree to which the current literature fits the proposed theoretical model ( Anderson et al., 2011 , Buelvas et al., 2013 ). Articles were searched in multiple data bases using a search strategy described in Section 3.2 . Once the articles were collected they were reviewed and organised using the SCS.
The scope of this review was restricted to peer-reviewed journal articles as a control on the quality and rigour across different studies in the area of MPDD. The search strategy comprised of two separate steps: (1) investigating literature reviews/meta-analyses and (2) analysing original research articles. A search was undertaken to identify literature reviews/meta-analyses on MPDD published in the 10 years 2005–2014. The last comprehensive literature review manuscript on the topic, that enabled the capture of prior research into the proposed MPDD framework of this study, was published by Collet et al., 2010a , Collet et al., 2010b . Therefore, the search for original research articles (i.e. not reviews/meta-analyses) was restricted to journal papers published between January 2010 and April 2015.
3.1. Systematic Classification Scheme (SCS)
The SCS is based on the integrative HMS framework developed for MPDD, which was adapted from the methodologies developed by Hachicha and Ghorbel, 2012 , Lage Junior and Godinho Filho, 2010 . It includes a series of questions intended to summarise all HMS components and interactions considered in published articles on MPDD. In particular, the proposed classification scheme helped map the kinds of variation in HMS components, the types of interactions between humans and mobile phones, or of the different driving performance metrics that were used in earlier research. The methodological and technological parameters of the research approaches were also structured by the SCS, not only to generalize the research findings but also to identify limitations and future research directions. The SCS adopted in this study consists of the following six questions:

    I.
    What was the study design (e.g. simulator studies, naturalistic studies)?
    II.
    What variations in the HMS components (e.g. driver demographics, type of mobile phone use) were included?
    III.
    What human-machine in-vehicle interactions (e.g. human-mobile phone interactions, human-car interactions) in MPDD were examined?
    IV.
    What interference associations between in-vehicle tasks (e.g. changes in steering control due to a mobile phone task) were described?
    V.
    What system performance metrics (e.g. speed, headway, lateral control) were utilized?
    VI.
    What associations between in-vehicle interaction tasks and system performance metrics (e.g. effects of mobile phone conversations or texting on speed and lateral control) were considered?

3.2. Search strategy
All searches included the word “driving” as mandatory, followed by the terms “mobile phone”, “cell phone”, “cellular”, or “telephone”, and “distraction”, or “interruption”. These terms were sought in the full text of the manuscripts. Studies explaining the prevalence of MPDD from a social, legal, economic or psychological point of view, and proposals to intervene in the use of a mobile phone while driving, were excluded.
4. Research on mobile phone distracted driving
This section compiles research on MPDD collected from two types of studies: review/meta-analysis studies, and original research articles. Following the structure of the proposed SCS, Section 4.1 systemically describes the findings from the past review studies and Section 4.2 presents the findings from original research articles published between 2010 and April 2015.
4.1. A Synthesis of Literature reviews/meta-analyses published from 2005 to 2014
Several literature reviews/meta-analyses have studied the relationship between mobile phone distraction and road safety. From 2004 to April 2015, a total of 11 literature reviews/meta-analyses have been published. They included five literature reviews ( Brace et al., 2007 , Collet et al., 2010a , Collet et al., 2010b , McCartt et al., 2006 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ), four meta-analyses ( Caird et al., 2014a , Caird et al., 2014b , Caird et al., 2008 , Horrey and Wickens, 2006 ), one systematic review ( Ferdinand and Menachemi, 2014 ), and one review of state-of-knowledge ( Ranney, 2008 ). The main characteristics of these articles are summarized in Table 3 . The number of articles reviewed in these literature review/meta-analysis papers ranged from 5 to 165.

