4.1.1. What was the study design?
As shown in Table 3 , past literature reviews/meta-analyses have summarized findings from various types of studies including analysis of traffic crashes (Crash analysis), controlled on-road studies on a pre-set route with an instrumented car (Instrumented vehicle), observation of drivers in a uncontrolled road traffic environment (Naturalistic observation), simulation of specific in-vehicle task in a laboratory (Part-task simulation), simulated driving (Driving simulators), and use of self-reported data (Survey). Study designs are very important for analysing the impacts of MPDD on safety. Key advantages of driving simulator studies are: control of driving parameters ( Svenson and Patten, 2005 ), economic feasibility ( Brace et al., 2007 ), and safer conditions for the participants ( Collet et al., 2010b ) in contrast to naturalistic driving studies. However, driving simulator studies are often criticized for the lack of realism compared to real world driving where drivers can engage a variety of strategies such as pull over the vehicle to attend a secondary task ( Caird et al., 2014b , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ). In addition, task driving conditions such as the use of uncommon conversations or verbalized simulator protocols often raise questions about the validity of findings in the area of MPDD and safety ( Collet et al., 2010b ). Although naturalistic studies are believed to have higher accuracy and validity ( Caird et al., 2008 ), their small sample sizes often limit the generalization of findings ( McCartt et al., 2006 ). Survey/epidemiological approaches, on the other hand, are subject to inherent bias due to underreporting and information loss.
Factors like data collection strategies, sample sizes, and tools of analysis are seldom examined in the literature reviews. A common practice has been to pool the studies based on the methodological approach without distinguishing the differences among data collection tools ( Brace et al., 2007 , Caird et al., 2014a , Caird et al., 2014b , Caird et al., 2008 , Collet et al., 2010a , Collet et al., 2010b , Ferdinand and Menachemi, 2014 , Horrey and Wickens, 2006 , McCartt et al., 2006 , Ranney, 2008 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ). This may make the summarized results less reliable or comparable. Although Collet et al., 2010a , Collet et al., 2010b considered surveys and physiological measures as sources of information for summarizing the literature on MPDD, the technological differences and degrees of sophistication amongst studies were not discussed thoroughly. In addition to data collection strategies, the sample sizes of simulator studies and naturalistic driving studies are important and thus considered by many literature review studies on MPDD. McCartt et al. (2006) reported that the sample size ranged from 8 to 350 with a mean of 46 and median of 30 in driving simulator studies, a mean of 30 and median of 37 in on-road studies with instrumented vehicles, and a mean of 27 and median of 21 in naturalistic studies. To overcome the variability and limitations in sample sizes across research articles, meta-analyses are used to generalize findings on MPDD ( Caird et al., 2014a , Caird et al., 2014b , Caird et al., 2008 ), but the tools used for data analysis have received less attention in summarizing the findings by the literature reviews/meta-analyses.
4.1.2. What variations in the HMS components were included?
Among the four components of the HMS in MPDD, the most studied are drivers and their demographics. Driver age has been reported to be inversely related to the ability required to share attention in a dual task execution like MPDD ( Brace et al., 2007 ). However, as reported by McCartt et al. (2006) , a lack of defined age ranges for young (approximately 16–25 years) and old (approximately 40–80 years) drivers coupled with unconsidered factors like driving experience, and mobile phone usage history often hinder deriving conclusive findings. The reported effects of driver gender on MPDD and safety have also been mixed ( Brace et al., 2007 , Collet et al., 2010b , McCartt et al., 2006 ).
Literature reviews of MPDD have not reported on the influence of vehicles—the plant component of the HMS. McCartt et al. (2006) considered vehicle transmission type as one of the variables for analysing the literature on MPDD and reported that it was often not specified in the studies. Svenson and Patten (2005) concluded that driving stress—measured as a function of the heart rate—of mobile phone distracted drivers increased by a lesser amount among drivers with automatic gearshift transmissions compared to manual gearshifts.
The effect of mobile phones, the second plant in the HMS, has been considered in many literature reviews mainly with a focus on whether hands-free technologies have less impact on the driving performances compared to handheld mobile phones. Most of the literature review studies have concluded that the crash risk of hands-free conversations is not significantly different from that of handheld phones ( Brace et al., 2007 , McCartt et al., 2006 ). Brace et al., 2007 , McCartt et al., 2006 concluded that handheld dialling leads to less safe driving as well as faster but inaccurate dialling of mobile phones. Horrey and Wickens, 2006 , Caird et al., 2008 showed that the impairment of driving performance for a hands-free phone conversation is equivalent to that experienced as the result of an in-vehicle conversation. Svenson and Patten (2005) argued that the position of the mobile phone in the car could interfere with the in-vehicle tasks and needs to be investigated. The effects of other phone characteristics like the size and type of the mobile phone on MPDD are usually not available in the literature ( McCartt et al., 2006 ).
The effects of road traffic environment on MPDD have been reported in a number of literature review studies ( Collet et al., 2010a , McCartt et al., 2006 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ). These studies suggest that heavy traffic delays the execution of any secondary task in addition to decreasing the cognitive function of memory. However, there are discrepancies in findings about the impact of environmental complexity on MPDD ( Brace et al., 2007 ).
4.1.3. What human-machine in-vehicle interactions in MPDD were studied?
As discussed previously, the proposed systematic framework for MPDD identified five possible in-vehicle interactions: human-car interface, human-car control, human-mobile phone interface, human-mobile phone control, and human-environment. Table 3 includes various in-vehicle interactions considered by literature review studies. Mobile phone conversation (an interaction in the human-mobile phone interface) and braking pattern (an interaction in the human-car controls) were the most studied in-vehicle interactions. Some studies have also analysed the effects of concurrent use of mobile phone control and interfaces like texting ( Brace et al., 2007 , Caird et al., 2014a , Caird et al., 2014b ), answering ( Svenson and Patten, 2005 ), dialling using the keyboard ( Brace et al., 2007 , Caird et al., 2014b , Collet et al., 2010a , McCartt et al., 2006 ), and navigation ( Brace et al., 2007 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ) on MPDD. The simultaneous use of mobile phone controls and interfaces imposes a high workload on drivers and thus has been reported to represent a high risk situation. Although the effects of human-mobile phone interaction have been studied widely, the research on the varying effects of human-car interface interactions (e.g. reading the speedometer or odometer) on MPDD is relatively inexistent.
4.1.4. What interference associations among in-vehicle tasks were described?
The execution of any secondary task may have impacts on the performance of the primary driving task. The literature reviews and meta-analysis studies analysed how human-mobile interactions (secondary tasks) influence the primary driving task. The driving task was measured in terms of driver control of the car (human-car control) and driver reading of the road traffic situation (human-environment interaction). Table 4 summarizes the details of the effects of various human-mobile phone interactions on the ‘human-car control’ and ‘human-environment’ interactions. It appears that human-mobile phone interactions change the human-car control behaviour, leading to increases in both steering wheel corrections ( Brace et al., 2007 , Caird et al., 2014b , Collet et al., 2010a , McCartt et al., 2006 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ) and braking response times ( Brace et al., 2007 , Caird et al., 2014b , Caird et al., 2008 , Collet et al., 2010a , Collet et al., 2010b , Horrey and Wickens, 2006 , McCartt et al., 2006 , Ranney, 2008 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ). Mobile phone tasks were also reported to influence human-environment interactions, leading to a decrease in visual scanning of drivers. As seen in Table 3 , the effects of secondary tasks on human-car interfaces were not addressed in the literature reviews.

