4.1.5. What system performance metrics were utilized?
As reported in Table 3 , the literature reviews/meta-analyses studies summarized the research findings on MPDD on a variety of system outcomes including functionality and various non-functional properties like occupancy, safety, and serviceability . Functionality was included in the form of speed selection, lateral position maintenance, and headway distance . Occupancy was measured in terms of driver’s subjective workload, and Safety was measured in terms of the prevalence of crashes. Serviceability of MPDD was measured by drivers’ memory utilization and information processing .
4.1.6. What associations between in-vehicle interaction tasks and system performance metrics were described?
Table 5 presents an analysis of the literature review studies to summarize the impact of various human-mobile phone interactions on system performance metrics. The impact of mobile phone conversation on system performance is the most studied topic in the literature review studies in contrast to other tasks, and has been reported to influence almost all of the system performance metrics including functionality ( Brace et al., 2007 , Caird et al., 2014b , Caird et al., 2008 , Collet et al., 2010a , McCartt et al., 2006 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ), occupancy ( Brace et al., 2007 , Caird et al., 2008 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ), safety ( Caird et al., 2014a , Caird et al., 2014b , Collet et al., 2010b , Ranney, 2008 , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ), and serviceability ( Collet et al., 2010b , Svenson and Patten, 2005 ). The analysis of the literature review articles suggested that conversing/dialling/texting while driving results in a reduction of driving speed but an increase in headways. However their effects on lane position are mixed.

