4.2. Synthesis of literature from 2010 to April 2015
Following the search strategy described in Section 3.2 , 62 journal articles were identified as being published on the topic of MPDD between January 2010 and April 2015. All 62 articles were critically reviewed and have been mapped onto the developed systematic classification scheme (SCS).
4.2.1. What was the study design?
Identified articles were categorized according to their study design, type of tool applied, and analytic method used to deduce the research findings (see Fig. A1 in the appendix). Use of a driving simulator was the most implemented study design for investigating MPDD, accounting for about 63% of the studies on the topic. Simulator studies were more frequently conducted in custom built simulators or desktop simulators (61%) than high fidelity simulators (37%). Naturalistic studies represent 12% of the studies on MPDD, but about 63% of naturalistic studies were published in 2014–2015, representing a recent research effort in this area.
The sample size of a study, which influences the statistical power of analyses, generally depends on the type of study and experimental design . The average numbers of participants were 3043 in cohort studies , 1793 in naturalistic studies, 1248 in crash analyses, 56 in quasi-naturalistic studies, 46 in part-task simulations, and 39 in driving simulator studies. The analytical methods varied across the studies depending on the nature of the data and objectives. In particular, 44% of articles applied analysis of variance (ANOVA) to determine the differences between distracted and non-distracted driving performance and other parameters of interest. The study of MPDD has not generally been oriented to the analysis of complex interactions that require the use of modelling techniques and such studies are still scarce in the literature.
4.2.2. What variations in the HMS components were included?
Drivers were the most studied system component among the four components of the HMS in MPDD, as more than 84% of articles reported controlling for driver age or examining the effect of driver age on MPDD. In particular, about 11% of articles examined various performance differences under distracted driving across age groups, summarized in Table 6 . Older drivers tend to engage less in a secondary task like using mobile phones while driving ( Becic et al., 2010 , Liu and Ou, 2011 , Owens et al., 2011 , Tractinsky et al., 2013 ). In contrast, the performance of younger drivers, who are inclined to use mobile phone while driving ( Reimer et al., 2011 ), has been reported to be less affected by mobile phone tasks than older drivers ( Asbridge et al., 2013 ). Many studies have also reported a negligible effect of age difference across various performance measures including speed selection ( Reimer et al., 2011 ), stop sign pauses ( Reimer et al., 2011 ), gaze behaviour ( Reimer et al., 2012 ), driving task performances ( Stavrinos et al., 2013 ), and risky driving behaviours ( Zhao et al., 2013 ). Driver gender was also not a significant factor in many MPDD studies (e.g., Zhao et al. (2013) ). The effects of driving experience on MPDD have been rarely studied. While Tractinsky et al. (2013) suggested that experienced drivers performed consistently better while driving under a secondary task, Stavrinos et al. (2013) did not find any effect of driving experience on MPDD.

