4.2.6. What associations between in-vehicle interaction tasks and system’s performance metrics were found?
Functionality: Recent literature has examined various functionality parameters of the HMS in the context of MPDD, including speed, acceleration, lane position, and headway distance. The speed selection of drivers has been reported to be influenced by various types of mobile phone tasks, including conversation ( Becic et al., 2010 , Metz et al., 2015 , Reimer et al., 2011 , Tractinsky et al., 2013 , Yannis et al., 2010 ), holding a mobile phone ( Christoph et al., 2013 ), navigation ( Christoph et al., 2013 ), reading ( Rudin-Brown et al., 2013 ), reaching to a mobile phone ( Christoph et al., 2013 ), texting ( McKeever et al., 2013 , Thapa et al., 2014 ), answering by pressing the send button ( Tractinsky et al., 2013 ), and dialling ( Tractinsky et al., 2013 ). While a majority of studies reported a decrease in speed selection under MPDD, some reported an increase in speed for mobile phone conversation ( Garrison and Williams, 2013 , Liu and Ou, 2011 , Stavrinos et al., 2013 ), and texting ( Rudin-Brown et al., 2013 , Young et al., 2014 ). Speed variability has been reported to increase if the conversation includes emotional components ( Dula et al., 2011 ). A study matching self-reported behaviour and observed driving performance found that drivers who reported frequent use of a mobile phone while driving changed speed more rapidly with faster throttle accelerations, and sudden non-directional accelerations ( Zhao et al., 2013 ). Platten et al. (2013) reported that distracted drivers approaching hazardous situations decreased speed rapidly with higher decelerations.
Distracted drivers have been reported to have less lane deviation while conversing ( Garrison and Williams, 2013 , Reimer et al., 2014 ) but an increased deviation while texting ( McKeever et al., 2013 , Rudin-Brown et al., 2013 ) compared to non-distracted drivers. Many studies, however, have reported a negligible difference in lane position between the baseline and the distractive conditions ( Cao and Liu, 2013 , Irwin et al., 2015 , Young et al., 2014 ). In terms of headway maintenance, distracted drivers have been reported to maintain a longer following distance ( Bergen et al., 2013 , Yannis et al., 2010 ) and to have more gap variations ( He et al., 2014 ). Pouyakian et al. (2013) reported that drivers answer mobile phone calls more frequently when headway distance is greater than 25 m.
Occupancy: System occupancy refers to the utilization of resources in the system, which is mostly measured by the workload of the drivers in the MPDD context. Various subjective or thorough physiological measures have been used to measure driver workload, including eye movement, skin conductance ( Reimer et al., 2012 ), skin temperature, and heart rate ( Chen, 2013 ). Drivers workload (demand) has generally been found to increase in all the dual task conditions related to MPDD ( Soccolich et al., 2014 ). Several studies ( Owens et al., 2011 , Young et al., 2014 ) reported that drivers’ subjective workload is much higher in a driving while texting condition compared to other mobile phone tasks. Long exposure to driving with mobile phone distraction has been reported to decrease the workload perceived by the driver ( Arnold and Houten, 2011 ).
Quality: Quality of the HMS in the MPDD context refers to proficiency in mobility, which can be measured with travel time and traffic violations. Mobile phone conversation has been found to impair the quality of the system as drivers distracted by mobile phone conversations committed more driving errors and road violations, e.g. road lanes excursions, speeding, red light running ( Nabatilan et al., 2012 ). In contrast, Dula et al., 2011 , Platten et al., 2013 have not found any significant difference in the number of traffic light infractions for conversation tasks.
Safety: About 24% of the articles published from 2010 to April 2015 reported associations between MPDD and safety. Many epidemiological studies reported a strong causal link between crashes and the presence of a mobile phone in a car ( Asbridge et al., 2013 , Backer-Grøndahl and Sagberg, 2011 , Farmer et al., 2010 , Tivesten and Dozza, 2014 ). On the other hand, a decrease in crash risk due to mobile phone manipulation has been argued to be the effect of learning-adaptation of drivers ( Backer-Grøndahl and Sagberg, 2011 , Petzoldt et al., 2014 ). Farmer et al. (2010) also argued that there is no evidence as yet that the ban on mobile phones decreased crash rates in the United States. However, epidemiological approaches of this kind have some limitations such as under-reporting and misjudgement of the mobile phone exposure, and hence the results should be considered with due care ( Asbridge et al., 2013 ).
Empirical studies, in contrast, do not include high number of crashes and thus usually do not report on crash risks associated with MPDD. Among the few studies that have discussed safety, mobile phone tasks like dialling ( Tractinsky et al., 2013 ), and texting ( Alosco et al., 2012 , Bendak, 2014 , Kim et al., 2013 , Stavrinos et al., 2013 ) have been reported to increase the frequency of collisions. Activities that require drivers to take their eyes off the road result in a higher likelihood of crashes ( Hickman and Hanowski, 2012 ); the longer the off-road glance, the higher the resultant crash risk ( Simons-Morton et al., 2014 ).
Serviceability: The serviceability of MPDD includes the cognitive function of drivers required for performing various tasks including driving and using mobile phones. The literature analysing this support function has been mainly focused on memory utilization and the information processing capability of drivers distracted by mobile phones. Research has shown that language production and comprehension capability, measured as the accuracy of drivers storytelling task, decreases in the MPDD condition ( Atchley et al., 2011 ). Conversation performance like the encoding of products of comprehension into memory is also relatively more affected when a person is involved in driving ( Becic et al., 2010 ).
Resiliency: The overtaking manoeuver, a measure of resiliency which refers to how the system improves proficiency loss due to mobile phone distraction, was reported in one study in the recent literature. Stavrinos et al. (2013) reported that there was no significant association between distraction (mobile phone conversations or reading text messages) and cars the distracted drivers passed in a simulated network. However, there is a paucity of research whether or how a distracted driver performs an overtaking manoeuver.
5. Discussion and future research directions
This paper presents a novel systematic framework based on HMS with the intent to provide an in-depth and comprehensive understanding of the components and mechanism of MPDD. Although an understanding of the mediating factors is important for the effective design of countermeasures and for understanding the differences in driver populations, little research on these has been conducted ( Young and Regan, 2008 ). The ultimate aim of defining MPDD using the HMS framework is to properly understand how differences in the components affect the system and interactions between tasks and the system performance outcomes in order to identify where on the system breaches in performance are occurring, and how interventions and strategies can serve to improve safety.
At a more detailed level, the proposed approach helps explain how system components and their interactions affect in-vehicle tasks in the interface or controls of vehicle and the mobile phone in combination with road traffic environments. This explanation involves dividing in-vehicle tasks into sub or smaller tasks according to the use of the interfaces and controls, which make it possible to explain the complexities involved with these activities. For example, texting and driving, which requires a simultaneous use of mobile phone controls and interfaces by drivers, is a complex task.
The proposed framework also describes how dual in-vehicle tasks affect both driving and mobile phone task performance. Subsequently, the model explains how in-vehicle tasks affect road traffic system properties adapted from system lifecycle properties defined by de Weck et al. (2012) . The road traffic system properties include functionality, safety, serviceability , occupancy, quality and resiliency. The inclusion of system properties brings theoretical clarity on how MPDD affects various road traffic system properties, and identifies the research gaps, common issues and research needs in the area of MPDD. However, most of the studies that resulted from following the methodology studied behavioural issues on MPDD. This shows a lack of knowledge on these systemic interactions which potentially undermine efforts of practitioners and decision makers for understanding the impact of MPDD and designing robust countermeasures.
5.1. Research design
Driving simulator and naturalistic studies are common approaches to examine MPDD. In the literature, it has been argued that simulator studies still need to overcome their limitations in terms of ecological validity and driver engagement ( Leung et al., 2012 , McCartt et al., 2006 ). Despite these limitations, they will continue to be used mainly because of ethical and safety issues. However, many simulator studies in the area of MPDD have applied a reductionist methodology to examine the effects of a particular type of distraction (e.g., mobile phone conversation, texting, etc.) on driving performance. It is still not well understood how drivers manage in-vehicle secondary tasks and, not having this information on driver behavioural models may over- or underestimate the impact of MPDD. A simulator experiment could be useful to investigate when and how a driver engages in a secondary task like using a mobile phone while driving (strategic behavioural adaptation), and how they manage or compensate for the risk of distracted driving (operative and tactical behavioural adaptation), and the resultant effects on overall system performance. The measurement of different levels of behavioural adaptation could be employed for calibration of traffic behavioural models in order to measure its effect on system performance and supporting mobile phone-vehicle cooperation technologies that could potentially prevent unsafe risk compensatory strategies.
Advancements in technological capabilities for data collection and analysis have led to an increased number of naturalistic studies since 2010. Naturalistic studies address the limitation of simulator studies in terms of ecological validity and driver engagement and are likely to bring insights into the consequences of MPDD. However, they do not expose participants to the same road traffic situation or distractions. At the moment, the relative validity of simulator studies compared to on-road studies is unknown ( Collet et al., 2010b ), and remains an interesting topic for future research—the answer to which may assist researchers to better design a research methodology to understand the influence of MPDD on driving.
Small samples sizes and population bias are two common issues in the MPDD literature. The average sample size among articles published between 2010 and 2014 was about 39.4, whereas the average sample size among studies prior to 2010 was about 30.0 ( McCartt et al., 2006 )—suggesting that more recent efforts have tried to obtain larger samples. A future study focussing on the sensitivity of sample sizes may help researchers to determine optimal sample sizes for their research designs. Another point of contention is potential population bias in research design. As argued by Alosco et al. (2012) , the research findings on distracted driving could be different if the participants were not “healthy young adults”. It is a common belief that driving distraction effects are likely to be different across individuals depending on their cognitive capacity, driving exposure, health condition and various human behaviour factors like sensation seeking and aggressiveness. While there is much potential for research in this area, an emerging and related research need is to examine how vocational drivers like ambulance drivers, policemen, and taxi drivers manage in-vehicle communication tasks while driving, given that these driver groups are regularly exposed to distracted driving within their job responsibilities.
5.2. HMS components
Among the three components of MPDD, the most studied components are drivers and their demographics. The effects of driver age and gender on MPDD are mixed, but the experience of distracted driving may play a major role in maintaining a proficient performance while driving ( Asbridge et al., 2013 , Tractinsky et al., 2013 ). The safety implications of these findings are enormous. For instance, intelligent support systems able to detect distracted driving based on control parameters may fail to detect this particular group of experienced subjects. In order to increase the robustness of distraction detection systems, future research should focus on enhancing systems architectures through physiological and neural responses to distraction.
With regard to the modifications in the plant (i.e. mobile phone, car) of the proposed HMS, different types of mobile phone use have been studied in the literature. The most studied research question is whether hands-free driving is safer than driving with a handheld phone. It is reported that both types of mobile phone use have similar crash risk compared to baseline or non-distracted driving ( Brace et al., 2007 , McCartt et al., 2006 , Soccolich et al., 2014 ). This result implies that the utilization of cognitive resources by drivers is the most important factor in the performance of driving tasks. The change in the mobile phone interface from keyboards to touch-screens has been reported to lengthen the off-road glances of drivers ( Reimer et al., 2014 ), which may eventually increase the crash risk of distracted driving ( Simons-Morton et al., 2014 ). Although various forms of mobile phone use have been studied, relatively little is known about how various vehicle features like steering control, manual/automatic gearshift, speedometer and other displays interact with mobile phone use and subsequent driving performance under the MPDD condition. Thus, the potential conflicts between mobile phone usage and vehicle features would be a worthwhile research pursuit. Potential technological advances could involve re-design of vehicle or mobile phone HMS plant to diminish potential conflicts. The road traffic environment plays a major role in MPDD ( Fitch et al., 2014 ), but its effects have not been well studied, as the main focus of the majority of studies has been to examine the performance of distracted drivers across road traffic environments such as rural roads , highways, and urban roads. There remains a research gap in understanding how different road traffic scenarios impact vehicle control under distracted driving conditions. A lack of consistent definitions of traffic environment complexity has led researchers to consider the complexity of the road traffic environment intuitively. A theory-based approach could be used to define road traffic environment complexity and avoid overrepresentation of low probability events and complex manoeuvres. This may also help researchers to design better scenarios for the driving simulator to investigate how environmental complexity modifies the driving performance of distracted drivers and motivates their decisions to engage in distracting tasks like mobile phone conversations and texting. These results could also be used to create a more efficient and safer cooperative driving environment, since responses to mobile phone distracted driving could be calibrated on an environment-situation basis.
5.3. In-vehicle tasks
In-vehicle tasks examined include braking pedal and steering wheel control. Yet, other interactions such as accelerator pedal control and information procurement from the car interface appear to have been largely neglected in the literature. For instance, reading the vehicle speed from speedometer plays an important role in vehicle safety. If a direct impact on this task is found, research is needed on the design of safer vehicle interfaces including distraction as one of the design variables. Further research should include possible interference between driver-car interface tasks and mobile phone use. The lack of knowledge regarding these interactions prevents us from understanding their impact on driving performance and developing technology-based countermeasures (e.g., a real-time distraction monitoring system based on vehicle dynamics and eye/head movements of distracted drivers).
In contrast, in-vehicle mobile phone tasks include a large typology such as texting, conversing, navigating, and answering, among many others. Until now, the focus has mainly been on the conversation task; however, activities like conversing require the execution of other supporting tasks including dialling, answering, monitoring the battery and reaching for the phone. Research in this area must be able to consider these elements to provide a more realistic representation of the extent of MPDD. Another interesting opportunity for research is to understand the effects of ringing, navigation and dialling tasks.
5.4. In-vehicle tasks interference
The execution of in-vehicle tasks results in a two-way interaction between driving and using mobile phones. For instance, texting and conversations affect drivers car control including slower speed selection (acceleration pedals), variability in lateral position (steering wheel), and increased braking times. Simultaneously, MPDD influences the performance of mobile phone tasks as evidenced by language production rates and less accurate cognitive processing . Some researchers have suggested that this phenomenon may represent risk compensation behaviour of distracted drivers ( Becic et al., 2010 , Tractinsky et al., 2013 ). However, a well-designed experiment is needed to isolate each of these interference components so that the risk compensation of drivers may be traced and evaluated. This would allow development of a holistic model to explain distracted driving and help in finding technological solutions to minimize interference with driving tasks.
5.5. System performance metrics
The proposed system performance metrics allow measuring the impact of MPDD on the traffic system comprehensively. It is quite evident that MPDD does not only influence safety but also mobility in terms of quality and resiliency, implying that the performance of distracted drivers is impaired and they commit more driving errors and road violations. In addition, mobile phone distractions also decrease the cognitive function of drivers and distracted drivers often perceive higher levels of workload, which are respectively related to the system serviceability and occupancy. This review shows that most of the research has been focused on safety and functionality although MPDD has consequences on other system properties (e.g., serviceability, occupancy, etc.) as demonstrated by the findings of this study. There is a clear need for fundamental research to develop a comprehensive model that takes into account all system properties and quantifies the effect of MPDD on system performance metrics.
5.6. Associations between in-vehicle tasks and system performance metrics
Most of the relationships between in-vehicle tasks and the outcomes of the traffic system remain unclear in the literature. The most studied systemic outcome is functionality. So far, the majority of results show a significant decrease in speed while conversing on a mobile phone but this result was not as robust as it was for texting. In other functionality measures, lane position and headway have often led to conflicting results about the direction of the effect, but there is a difference between non-distracted and distracted conditions.
The bulk of the literature has so far offered conflicting and inconsistent results concerning the causal relationship between mobile phone distracted driving and system safety. Functionality parameters have been used as alternative indicator of safety of MPDD, yet this approach needs to be investigated in depth since the causal associations, if they exist, remain undefined ( Becic et al., 2010 ). Recent naturalistic studies have not found changes in the crash likelihood in the conversation activity ( Fitch et al., 2014 , Hickman and Hanowski, 2012 ), while the opposite happens with texting and in general all in-vehicle tasks that require longer off-road glances ( Simons-Morton et al., 2014 ). These data must be interpreted with caution, because naturalistic studies have restrictions in identifying the actual interactions with the road traffic environment and drivers moods ( Metz et al., 2015 ). In contrast, while a strong causal association between texting and crashes has been reported, the results have not been consistent for conversations. Undoubtedly, longer off-road glances due to using the interfaces and controls of mobile phones play a vital role. In addition, more demanding texting tasks seem to have a greater impact on system safety, and specifically on the prevalence of collisions. Similarly, all activities that require drivers to take their eyes off the road result in higher likelihood of crashes ( Hickman and Hanowski, 2012 ); the longer the off-road glance, the higher the resulting crash risk ( Alosco et al., 2012 , Simons-Morton et al., 2014 ). Technology-based countermeasures to provide real-time feedback based on eye behaviour could be a solid countermeasure for MPDD.
6. Conclusions
This study describes the mechanisms in which mobile phone interaction affects the driving task and system performance. To the authors’ knowledge, this is the first attempt in which a systemic approach has been developed for synthesizing the literature on mobile phone distracted driving. The results provide an understanding of the empirical relationships observed in the MPDD literature—a literature that is full of controversial and divergent results. In this paper, a total of 75 documents were reviewed, covering literature reviews, meta-analysis and original research articles over a time span of 10  years.
Overall, the results of this study provide significant insight into the four fundamental issues surrounding MPDD. Firstly, it provides a framework that lists system components and defines their complex associations. However, it must be acknowledged that variations of the MPDD as a HMS can be found in real life applications, for example, presence of passengers in the car who may support the driver in using the mobile phone. These variations could be included in the HMS as new components and their interaction can be modelled. Secondly, it creates a system framework that allows for conceptual organization and prioritization of a variety of different performance indicators used in the literature on MPDD. Thirdly, it explains the phenomenon of compensation and helps identify its components in the MPDD model. Finally, it summarizes the current state of scientific progress in this research area and provides a map for future research.
In summary, a systematic framework that considers all of the components and interactions associated with MPDD will help to explain the risk compensation behaviour of drivers. It will accommodate the potential endogeneity within the relationship, and will enable greater insights than have been afforded to date. Our future efforts shall focus on implementing this analysis framework to achieve these aims.
